		<!-- Section 4: Spotify, Audible & Libby: How Listening Time Limits & Percentages Work -->
		<section class="mx-auto max-w-[880px] space-y-6 border-t border-hairline px-4 pt-12 pb-16 sm:px-6 md:pt-16">
			<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				Spotify, Audible &amp; Libby: How Listening Time Limits &amp; Percentages Work
			</h2>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Every major audiobook platform tracks listening hours and completion percentages slightly differently. Understanding how quota rules and progress bars work on <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Spotify</strong>, <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Audible</strong>, <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Kindle</strong>, and <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Libby</strong> helps you avoid mid-chapter cutoffs and log accurate progress in Goodreads or StoryGraph.
			</p>

			<div class="grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
				<!-- Card 1: Spotify 15-Hour Monthly Quota -->
				<div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-5 space-y-3">
					<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
						1. Spotify Audiobook Listening Time Limit (15-Hour Monthly Quota)
					</h3>

					<p class="text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Spotify includes <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">15 hours of audiobook listening per billing month</strong> with <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Spotify Premium Individual</strong>, <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Spotify Duo</strong>, and <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Spotify Family</strong> plans. On Duo and Family subscriptions, only the primary <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">plan manager</strong> receives the included 15-hour monthly allowance—secondary sub-accounts do not get separate hours unless purchased as 10-hour top-ups.
					</p>

					<p class="text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Crucial playback speed rule:</strong> Spotify deducts from your 15-hour quota based on the book's <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">original 1.0x catalog length</strong>, not your actual wall-clock listening time. Listening to a 15-hour audiobook at <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">1.5x speed</strong> takes you 10 hours in real life, but it still consumes all <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">15 hours</strong> of your monthly Spotify allowance.
					</p>

					<p class="text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">How to check Spotify audiobook listening time left:</strong> Open the Spotify mobile app and tap <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Profile picture &rarr; Settings and privacy &rarr; Account &rarr; Subscription &rarr; Track your included hours</strong>. Once you see your remaining quota, enter those hours into the <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Remaining Progress</strong> or <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Daily listening schedule</strong> panel above to plan your exact finish date before your billing cycle resets.
					</p>
				</div>

				<!-- Card 2: Audible, Kindle & Libby Percentage Read -->
				<div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-5 space-y-3">
					<h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
						2. Audiobook Percentage Calculator for Audible, Kindle &amp; Libby
					</h3>

					<p class="text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Readers frequently search <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">how to find percentage on Audible</strong> or check their <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Audible percentage read</strong> when updating reading trackers. While <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Kindle Whispersync</strong> and library apps like <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Libby</strong> prominently display completion percentage (<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">%</strong>) on the main player screen, Audible's mobile player focuses on <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">chapter time remaining</strong> and <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">total time left</strong> (with overall percentage tucked inside your web library or book details view).
					</p>

					<p class="text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Instead of digging through menus or guessing your progress, open the <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Remaining Progress</strong> tab in the <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">audiobook percentage calculator</strong> above. It works in both directions:
					</p>

					<ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						<li>
							<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Time Left &rarr; Percentage:</strong> Enter your total book length and Audible's <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Remaining time (at 1.0x)</strong> to get your exact <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">% completed</strong> for Goodreads or StoryGraph.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Percentage &rarr; Hours &amp; Minutes Left:</strong> Type or slide your <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Percentage completed (%)</strong> from Libby or Kindle to see your exact elapsed time and adjusted time remaining at <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">1.25x</strong>, <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">1.5x</strong>, or <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">2.0x</strong> speed.
						</li>
					</ul>
