		<!-- Section 5: How to Choose the Best Audiobook Playback Speed Without Losing Comprehension -->
		<section class="mx-auto max-w-[880px] space-y-6 border-t border-hairline px-4 pt-12 pb-16 sm:px-6 md:pt-16">
			<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				How to Choose the Best Audiobook Playback Speed Without Losing Comprehension
			</h2>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Cognitive psychology research shows that the human brain can comfortably process and comprehend spoken language at <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">250 to 300 words per minute (WPM)</strong>. However, professional studio narration at <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">1.0x</strong> is deliberately recorded around <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">150 to 160 WPM</strong> for maximum vocal clarity. That 100-WPM gap is why 1.0x narration can sometimes let your mind wander—and why <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">1.25x to 1.5x (~194–233 WPM)</strong> is the natural sweet spot for most listeners using an <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">audiobook playback speed calculator</strong>.
			</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				3-Step Speed-Up Training Method
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				If you want to build a faster listening habit using an <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">audiobook speed up calculator</strong> workflow without sacrificing retention, train your ear gradually:
			</p>

			<ol class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
				<li class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-5">
					<div class="mb-2 flex items-center gap-2.5">
						<span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-hairline text-[12px] font-semibold text-ink tabular-nums">1</span>
						<h4 class="text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">Warm Up at 1.15x–1.25x</h4>
					</div>
					<p class="text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Start at <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">1.15x or 1.25x</strong> for the first 10 minutes of a new book so your auditory cortex adapts to the narrator's voice, cadence, and pitch.
					</p>
				</li>

				<li class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-5">
					<div class="mb-2 flex items-center gap-2.5">
						<span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-hairline text-[12px] font-semibold text-ink tabular-nums">2</span>
						<h4 class="text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">Step Up by 0.10x</h4>
					</div>
					<p class="text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Increase playback in <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">0.10x increments</strong> (<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">1.25x &rarr; 1.35x &rarr; 1.5x</strong>) across chapters rather than jumping straight from 1.0x to 2.0x.
					</p>
				</li>

				<li class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-5">
					<div class="mb-2 flex items-center gap-2.5">
						<span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-hairline text-[12px] font-semibold text-ink tabular-nums">3</span>
						<h4 class="text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">Match Speed to Genre</h4>
					</div>
					<p class="text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Keep dense philosophy, sci-fi world-building, or unfamiliar accents at <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">1.0x–1.2x</strong>, while pushing conversational memoirs and business books to <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">1.5x–1.75x</strong>.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>

			<div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-5">
				<h3 class="text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
					The Daily Habit Compound Effect (33+ Books a Year)
				</h3>
				<p class="mt-1.5 text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
					Small playback adjustments compound dramatically over a year. Listening for just <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">45 minutes per day at 1.25x</strong> (which covers 56 minutes of 1.0x audio during a daily commute or walk) finishes a standard 10-hour audiobook in <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">11 days</strong>—adding up to <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">33+ books per year</strong> without carving out a single extra hour from your schedule.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

