				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>How do I use an audiobook percentage calculator and audiobook length calculator together?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						To calculate your completion percentage, divide your elapsed listening time by the total audiobook length and multiply by 100 (<code class="rounded bg-canvas px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[13.5px] text-ink tabular-nums">(Elapsed &divide; Total) &times; 100</code>), then divide the remaining time by your playback speed to find your adjusted length left. Or open the <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Remaining Progress</strong> tab in this <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">audiobook percentage calculator</strong> and <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">audiobook length calculator</strong> to enter either your percentage completed or elapsed time and convert both ways automatically.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>Does Audible show percentage completed, and how can I find it?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Audible's mobile player screen displays chapter time remaining and overall time left rather than a prominent percentage badge. You can check your completion percentage in Audible's web library, via Kindle Whispersync, or by entering your total length and time left into this calculator's <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Remaining Progress</strong> tab.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>Does listening at 1.5x or 2x save my Spotify 15-hour audiobook limit?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						No. Spotify deducts from your monthly <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">15-hour audiobook listening limit</strong> (included with Premium Individual, Duo plan managers, and Family plan managers) based on the book's <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">original 1.0x runtime</strong>, regardless of playback speed.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>How do I check how many audiobook hours I have left on Spotify?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
